\section{Implementation and Confidence Gates}
\label{sec:direction-config}

Table~\ref{tab:direction-implementation-gates} records the direction-specific
settings used by the reported simulations.  These thresholds are validity and
hysteresis safeguards rather than reference-trajectory fit parameters.  They
belong to the direction subsystem because they govern the carrier tracker,
axial acceptance, and travel-sense decision only.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Direction-estimator implementation defaults.}
\label{tab:direction-implementation-gates}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.55\columnwidth}
                    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.34\columnwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Setting & Default \\
\midrule
KalmANF carrier policy & enabled \\
carrier tracker band & $[0.2,6.0]$ Hz \\
frequency smoothing, fast/slow & $3.5/10$ s \\
axis amplitude/confidence/linearity gates & $0.08/20/0.25$ \\
travel-sense coherence, on/off & $0.20/0.12$ \\
travel-sense RMS floors, horizontal/vertical slope &
$0.01\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}/0.01\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-3}}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The axial estimator reports no current direction unless amplitude, covariance
confidence, and linearity pass simultaneously.  The travel-sense stage has its
own absolute-energy and coherence requirements and may therefore remain
unresolved even while the axis is valid.  This separation is intentional: a
well-determined propagation plane is not sufficient evidence for choosing one
of its two directed senses.
